@(tejvn) @(MaxA)&#x20;

**TL;DR**: If your distribution is roughly bell-shaped, and you assign 15-23% probability to Lula winning, then your distribution on this question should be roughly flat on \[0%, 3%].

Now that I think about it, the reason why my forecast is so flat from 0-3% is because the second derivative of my forecast is approximately 0 at x\=0 as seen in the orange plot (18% Lula, close to my 15%). This coincidence occurs because I assigned an 14% chance to Lula winning, and this roughly causes the second derivative to disappear. Most bell-shaped distributions have a point of inflection near the 15-23th percentile, so most bell-shaped forecasts roughly consistent with Polymarket's 15% odds of Lula winning should have a similar flat region. (The connection between having a point of inflection in your signed distribution near x \= 0% and having a longer flat region boils down to Taylor Series.)

For other probabilities of Lula winning (blue and green plots), the distribution remains roughly flat on \[0%, 1.5%] instead of \[0%, 3%].&#x20;

![](https://cdn.metaculus.com/user_uploaded/Screenshot_2026-10-09_at_2.47.23AM.png)Plot generated by Claude

@(MaxA)&#x20;
Agreed. On its own, the argument only addresses the distribution at x \= 0%, and the flatness of my forecast across 0–3% is a coincidence.&#x20;

Still, the two conditions at zero (the folded density’s height is double the signed density’s height at zero, and its slope there is zero) significantly constrain the 0–2% range, provided one accepts that the signed distribution should be roughly bell-shaped and representable with 1-2 Metaculus sliders (which I find to be reasonable and the community seems to believe). In fact, the current Community Prediction can be represented almost perfectly with 1 slider (shown below). I personally used 2 sliders, and found the two constraints at x\=0 helpful in constructing my forecast.

If your signed distribution significantly deviates from a bell shape and you're using 3 or more sliders (or the API) to represent your forecast, then my comments in this thread may not be as relevant.

